      defaults={["Python asyncio", "Rust / Tokio", "Go (goroutines)", "Rust / smol"]}
    />

    <p class="mt-4 text-xs text-brand-muted">
      These snippets are illustrative: each async runtime is an external crate, so it needs the matching dependency in <code class="rounded bg-gray-100 px-1 py-0.5 font-mono text-[11px]">Cargo.toml</code> (<code class="rounded bg-gray-100 px-1 py-0.5 font-mono text-[11px]">tokio</code>, <code class="rounded bg-gray-100 px-1 py-0.5 font-mono text-[11px]">smol</code>, <code class="rounded bg-gray-100 px-1 py-0.5 font-mono text-[11px]">glommio</code>). Only the <code class="rounded bg-gray-100 px-1 py-0.5 font-mono text-[11px]">std::thread</code> version is self-contained; <code class="rounded bg-gray-100 px-1 py-0.5 font-mono text-[11px]">glommio</code> additionally requires Linux <code class="rounded bg-gray-100 px-1 py-0.5 font-mono text-[11px]">io_uring</code>, so it will not run in the standard Rust playground.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- ====== PIPELINE DIAGRAM ====== -->
  <section class="mb-14">
    <p class="mono-eyebrow mb-2">// from source to execution</p>
    <h2 class="glitch mb-4 text-xl font-semibold text-brand-text" data-text="The Big Picture">The Big Picture</h2>
    <p class="mb-6 text-brand-body">
      All execution models are variations on the same pipeline. The key question is <em>when</em> compilation happens and <em>how many layers</em> sit between your source code and the CPU.
    </p>

    <figure class="rounded-xl border border-brand-border bg-white p-4 sm:p-6">
